// ─── FAQ DATA ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
const FAQS = [
  {
    q: "What is Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) and when should I use it?",
    a: "SEM is a multivariate statistical technique that allows you to test complex relationships between observed and latent variables. It's ideal when you have multiple interrelated dependent variables, or when you need to model measurement error. You should use SEM when your research questions involve indirect effects, latent constructs, or comparing groups."
  },
  {
    q: "What's the difference between AMOS, Mplus, and R for SEM?",
    a: "AMOS is user-friendly with a graphical interface, great for beginners and those who prefer drawing path diagrams. Mplus is more flexible and handles categorical data better, but has a steeper learning curve. R (lavaan) is free and highly customizable, but requires coding. We can work in any of them – just tell us your preference."
  },
  {
    q: "How many participants do I need for SEM?",
    a: "General rules of thumb: 5-10 observations per estimated parameter, or a minimum of 200 cases for most models. For more complex models with many parameters, you may need 400+. We can help you run a power analysis to determine the sample size you need."
  },
  {
    q: "What are common fit indices and what thresholds should I aim for?",
    a: "Common indices: CFI (≥ .90 acceptable, .95+ good), TLI (≥ .90 acceptable), RMSEA (≤ .08 acceptable, .05+ good), SRMR (≤ .08 acceptable). But these are guidelines – we'll interpret them in the context of your model and field norms."
  },
  {
    q: "Can you help me fix a non-converging or negative variance model?",
    a: "Yes – we regularly troubleshoot convergence issues, negative error variances, and Heywood cases. We'll check your model specification, sample size, and starting values, and guide you to a well-identified model that converges properly."
  }
];

